{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"f880f0886c99a391","name":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/OceaniaStorm/units/card_unit_panther_a_zimm","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"PANTHER A ZIMMERIT","text":"After this unit operates, increase all its stats by how often it has operated.","flavorText":"The Panther was one of Germany’s main medium tanks in the late-war period, but even before it entered widespread service there were concerns that its armor might prove insufficient against future Soviet tank guns. In early 1943, this led to work on the more heavily armored Panther II, which was expected to weigh around seven tons more than the standard Panther. Zimmerit was a protective coating applied to German armored vehicles to prevent infantry from attaching magnetic anti-tank mines to their hulls. The idea emerged after Germany introduced its own magnetic Hafthohlladung grenade in 1942 and feared that the Allies might develop similar weapons. Following experiments with several solutions, Zimmer AG developed the specialized coating in 1943. Application to Panther tanks began in September 1943. Around 160 kg of Zimmerit could be required for a Panther, with the paste spread across vertical and sloped armor before being hardened and shaped into distinctive patterns. On 9 September 1944, Germany ordered the Zimmerit application to stop after rumors claimed it could catch fire when hit. Later tests disproved the danger, but production never resumed.","faction":"Germany","type":"tank","rarity":"Unique","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":2,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm_title","SourceString":"PANTHER A ZIMMERIT","LocalizedString":"PANTHER A ZIMMERIT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::tank","attack":2,"defense":3,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm_text","SourceString":"After this unit operates, increase all its stats by how often it has operated.","LocalizedString":"After this unit operates, increase all its stats by how often it has operated."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm_history","SourceString":"The Panther was one of Germany’s main medium tanks in the late-war period, but even before it entered widespread service there were concerns that its armor might prove insufficient against future Soviet tank guns. In early 1943, this led to work on the more heavily armored Panther II, which was expected to weigh around seven tons more than the standard Panther. Zimmerit was a protective coating applied to German armored vehicles to prevent infantry from attaching magnetic anti-tank mines to their hulls. The idea emerged after Germany introduced its own magnetic Hafthohlladung grenade in 1942 and feared that the Allies might develop similar weapons. Following experiments with several solutions, Zimmer AG developed the specialized coating in 1943. Application to Panther tanks began in September 1943. Around 160 kg of Zimmerit could be required for a Panther, with the paste spread across vertical and sloped armor before being hardened and shaped into distinctive patterns. On 9 September 1944, Germany ordered the Zimmerit application to stop after rumors claimed it could catch fire when hit. Later tests disproved the danger, but production never resumed.","LocalizedString":"The Panther was one of Germany’s main medium tanks in the late-war period, but even before it entered widespread service there were concerns that its armor might prove insufficient against future Soviet tank guns. In early 1943, this led to work on the more heavily armored Panther II, which was expected to weigh around seven tons more than the standard Panther. Zimmerit was a protective coating applied to German armored vehicles to prevent infantry from attaching magnetic anti-tank mines to their hulls. The idea emerged after Germany introduced its own magnetic Hafthohlladung grenade in 1942 and feared that the Allies might develop similar weapons. Following experiments with several solutions, Zimmer AG developed the specialized coating in 1943. Application to Panther tanks began in September 1943. Around 160 kg of Zimmerit could be required for a Panther, with the paste spread across vertical and sloped armor before being hardened and shaped into distinctive patterns. On 9 September 1944, Germany ordered the Zimmerit application to stop after rumors claimed it could catch fire when hit. Later tests disproved the danger, but production never resumed."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","hasBlitz":true,"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/PANTHER_A_ZIMMERIT.PANTHER_A_ZIMMERIT","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm_photo","SourceString":"A Panther Ausf. A covered with Zimmerit. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"A Panther Ausf. A covered with Zimmerit. Date unknown."},"blowUpExplosionNiagara":{"ObjectName":"NiagaraSystem'NS_ExplosionArmourM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Niagara/NS_ExplosionArmourM.36"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeMedium_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeMedium.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/Tanks/BP_AttackTank.BP_AttackTank_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"count","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_panther_a_zimm","owner":"card_unit_panther_a_zimm_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"K2Node_Event_defenderCard","type":"UBaseCardObje